    "id": "ANAT-001-000003-I",
    "subject": "Anatomy",
    "text": "What is a part of the embryo that exerts morphogenetic stimulus on its adjacent part called?",
    "explanation": "Any part of the embryo that exerts a morphogenetic stimulus on adjacent parts is known as the organizer. First level is the primary organizer that induces the development of new tissues. This newly developed tissue can then induce another tissue in turn to become the secondary organizer. Therefore, there is a sequence or chain of organizers called primary, secondary, and tertiary organizers. \r\nExamples of the three levels of organizers: \r\n• Primary organizer: e.g., Blastopore induces differentiation of notochord \r\n• Secondary organizer: e.g., Notochord induces the development of the neural tube (that forms the brain and spinal cord) \r\n• Tertiary organizer. e.g., Neural tube induces segmentation of paraxial mesoderm into somites",
