    "id": "ANAT-002-000008-I",
    "subject": "Anatomy",
    "text": "What is the normal site of implantation of the blastocyst?",
    "explanation": "The blastocyst normally implants in the functional layer of the uterine endometrium. In the secretory phase, three layers can be recognized in the endometrium: stratum compactum, stratum spongiosum, and stratum basale. The first two layers (stratum compactum and spongiosum) are together known as stratum functionale or the functional layer. If implantation does not occur, the functional layer is shed off during menstruation. If implantation occurs, the functional layer is retained as the decidua. \r\nTimeline of implantation: \r\n• 3-4 days after fertilization: The morula (12-16 cells) enters the uterus. \r\n• 4th day after fertilization: The advanced morula reaches the uterine lumen. \r\n• 4-5 days after fertilization: The early blastocyst stage is seen. \r\n• 6-7 days after fertilization: The blastocyst implants in the uterus. Implantation corresponds to 20-22 days of the menstrual cycle.",
    "marks": 4,
    "negative": 1,
    "references": "Inderbir Singh's Human Embryology - 11th Edition, Inderbir Singh, Pg 76\r\nGray's Anatomy: The Anatomical Basis of Clinical Practice, 41st Edition, Susan Standring PhD DSc, Pg 1306",
