    "id": "ANAT-002-000009-I",
    "subject": "Anatomy",
    "text": "What does the inner cell mass form during blastocyst formation?",
    "explanation": "The inner cell mass gives rise to the embryoblast, which forms the embryo proper. Around the 4th-5th day of development, the morula becomes the blastocyst. Fluid passes into the morula from the uterine cavity and converts it to a blastocyst with a fluid-filled cavity called blastocoele. This fluid partially separates the cells of the inner cell mass from the outer cell mass. \r\nCell masses in the blastocyst: \r\n• The outer cell mass is called the trophoblast and forms a major part of the placenta \r\n• The inner cell mass is called the embryoblast and forms the embryo proper",
